<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>LocalScribe.Onnx</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../LocalScribe.Core/LocalScribe.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Windows ML, which carries the Qualcomm provider, is used for every build made on Windows. It
    ships win-x64 as well as win-arm64, and on x64 the Qualcomm provider simply never appears and
    the planner falls back, which is the behaviour we want anyway. The switch is the build
    host, not the runtime identifier, because .NET does not pass a runtime identifier on to a
    referenced library: keyed on it, this project restored Windows ML and then compiled as if
    it had not.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseQnnRuntime Condition="'$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT'">true</UseQnnRuntime>
    <UseQnnRuntime Condition="'$(UseQnnRuntime)' == ''">false</UseQnnRuntime>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    On Windows on ARM, ONNX Runtime comes from Windows ML, not from the QNN package. One runtime
    then serves the NPU (Qualcomm's provider, fetched once through Windows ML's catalog) and the
    GPU (DirectML, built in), in one process. The QNN package and the DirectML package are
    separate builds of one native library and never load together, so before this the word scan
    could not leave the NPU for the GPU without a second process. See docs/model-trials.md.

    Windows ML ships its managed ONNX Runtime only for Windows target frameworks. These
    projects stay on plain net8.0, so the managed API comes from the ordinary package, at the
    version Windows ML's native runtime was built from (1.24): the managed side asks the native
    one for its own API version and a newer native runtime serves older callers. The catalog
    is reached through Windows ML's flat C API (WindowsMl.cs), which needs no WinRT projection.

    The precompiled Whisper build for X Elite was made for QNN 2.33 and ONNX Runtime 1.22, the
    pin this replaces. Windows ML's Qualcomm provider (QNN 2.51) opens it: a decoder step 9 ms
    against 10, the encoder 814 ms against 771 (2026-11-26).
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseQnnRuntime)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.AI.MachineLearning" Version="2.1.74" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Managed" Version="1.24.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(UseQnnRuntime)' == 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);WINDOWS_ML</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The other branch floats ahead on purpose. ONNX Runtime 1.22's macOS build aborts in its
    native teardown at process exit (mutex lock failed in libc++), which 1.29 fixes. The two
    branches never meet in one process: a build is either made on Windows, with Windows ML, or
    elsewhere without it. Any bump on either branch is judged the project's way: the doctor's
    diarize turn diff and align stride check before and after, because the diarization tuning
    is frozen and a runtime that moves a boundary by a hundredth of a second is a tuning
    change.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseQnnRuntime)' != 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime" Version="1.29.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
